\lispPackageChapter{livermore/xcsr}

The \texttt{livermore/xcsr} package is XCS with interval
predicates~\cite{wilson-2000}.  Each allele is a closed interval
$[\mathit{lower},\mathit{upper}]$ over a real input.

\section{Classes}

\lispClassSubsection{range-predicate}

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{lower}] The inclusive lower bound.
\item[\texttt{upper}] The inclusive upper bound.
\end{description}

\texttt{spread} is half the width.  \texttt{center} is the midpoint.  If
\texttt{lower} exceeds \texttt{upper}, the two are swapped first.

\lispClassSubsection{xcsr-learning-parameters}

A subclass of \texttt{learning-parameters} with extra slots:

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{problem-range}] The range of the input, default
  \texttt{(0.0 1.0)}.
\item[\texttt{covering-maximum}] How large a fraction of the range covering
  may add to both bounds.  Default $0.1$.
\item[\texttt{mutation-maximum}] How large a fraction of the range mutation
  may add or subtract from each bound.  Default $0.10$.
\item[\texttt{initial-spread-limit}] $s_0$ in Wilson's XCSR paper, the
  largest initial spread when covering.  Default $0.5$.
\end{description}

\lispClassSubsection{xcsr}

An XCS whose default predicate type is \texttt{range-predicate} and whose
learning parameters are \texttt{xcsr-learning-parameters}.

\section{Methods}

\texttt{match?} against a number is true when the number lies in the
interval.  \texttt{match?} against another range is true when this interval
contains the other.

\texttt{more-general?} is true when this interval strictly contains the
other.

\texttt{cover} builds, for each situation element, an interval centered on
that element with a random spread no larger than
\texttt{initial-spread-limit}.

\texttt{mutate} adds a random amount, bounded by \texttt{mutation-maximum}
times the spread, to each bound, clipped to the problem range.

\texttt{covering-score} is the width of the interval as a fraction of the
problem range.
